<header><p class="st-kicker">other · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Provisional Government of Assiniboia</h1><p class="st-meta">1869 CE – 1870 CE · capital Upper Fort Garry</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Métis-led government formed by Louis Riel at Red River in 1869, whose negotiations with Ottawa created the province of Manitoba.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1869 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> agreed to sell <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ruperts-land">Rupert's Land</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/canada">Canada</a> without consulting the people of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/red-river-colony">Red River Colony</a>. Alarmed by Canadian surveyors, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> formed a National Committee, turned back the incoming lieutenant governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-mcdougall">William McDougall</a>, and in November seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-fort-garry">Upper Fort Garry</a>. A provisional government followed in December, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a> as president from late that month.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Riel</a>'s government drew in English-speaking delegates through a convention that drafted a List of Rights, including provincial status, bilingual institutions and protection of land. It held prisoners from the Canadian party, and in March 1870 executed one of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-scott-orangeman">Thomas Scott</a>, an act that inflamed Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. Delegates sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> negotiated the terms that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba-act">Manitoba Act</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba-act">Manitoba Act</a> of May 1870 created the province. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-expedition-1870">Wolseley Expedition</a> arrived in August, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Riel</a> fled. Promised land for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> was largely lost, but Riel is now honoured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> as its founder.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1869 CE – 1870 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">North America and Oceania</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.90%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1869 CE – 1870 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Upper Fort Garry</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis Riel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Michif, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Canada</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Red River provisional government, Riel's provisional government</dd></dl></aside></div>
